    <article class="ba-body">
      <p>Ayurveda is a traditional system of wellness that developed in India more than 3,000 years ago. The word comes from Sanskrit: <i>ayur</i> (life) and <i>veda</i> (knowledge) — the same root as our name.</p><p>It looks at daily habits — food, sleep, movement and seasonal routines — as a whole. Herbs and botanical powders have always been part of those everyday routines.</p>
      <h2 id="highlights">Highlights</h2><ul class="ba-hl"><li>Ayurveda means “knowledge of life” in Sanskrit.</li><li>It focuses on daily routine (dinacharya) and seasonal routine (ritucharya).</li><li>Botanicals like Amla, Triphala and Ashwagandha appear throughout traditional texts.</li><li>In the U.S., Ayurvedic herbs are sold as dietary supplements, not medicines.</li></ul>
      <h2 id="s0">A whole-day approach</h2><p>Rather than one product or one moment, Ayurveda thinks in rhythms: what you do in the morning, what you eat at midday, how you wind down at night. That's why we talk about rituals — small, repeatable habits.</p><h2 id="s1">The idea of doshas</h2><p>Traditional Ayurveda describes three doshas — Vata, Pitta and Kapha — as patterns that shape how each person feels and functions. Many people use the idea as a gentle lens for choosing foods and routines.</p><h2 id="s2">Where herbs fit in</h2><p>Herbs are used as part of food and daily routine — stirred into warm water, milk or meals. Single-ingredient powders make it easy to know exactly what you're adding.</p>
      <h2 id="glance">At a glance</h2><div class="ba-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Botanical</th><th scope="col">Part used</th><th scope="col">Traditional time of day</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Moringa</td><td>Leaf</td><td>Morning</td></tr><tr><td>Amla</td><td>Fruit</td><td>Midday</td></tr><tr><td>Ashwagandha</td><td>Root</td><td>Evening</td></tr><tr><td>Triphala</td><td>Three fruits</td><td>Night</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
