      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2><details class="ba-faq"><summary>Is Ayurveda a replacement for medical care?</summary><p>No. Ayurvedic botanicals are not a substitute for professional medical advice. Speak with a healthcare professional before starting any supplement.</p></details><details class="ba-faq"><summary>How are Ayurvedic products regulated in the U.S.?</summary><p>Most are sold as dietary supplements, which follow FDA rules for dietary supplements rather than drug approval.</p></details><details class="ba-faq"><summary>What does the word Ayurveda mean?</summary><p>It comes from the Sanskrit words ayur (life) and veda (knowledge), so it is often described as “knowledge of life.”</p></details><details class="ba-faq"><summary>What are the three doshas?</summary><p>Vata, Pitta and Kapha — patterns traditional Ayurveda uses to describe how each person feels and functions. Many people treat the idea as a gentle lens for choosing foods and routines.</p></details>
